+++ b/vccl_alloc/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,199 @@
+# vccl_alloc
+
+A custom CUDA allocator that provides PyTorch tensors backed by NCCL-allocated
+memory, sliced into fixed-size slots. Designed for `ncclCommWindowRegister` /
+`ncclCommRegister`-eligible buffers in vccl-megatron's MoE alltoallv path.
+
+Each named pool gets its **own** NCCL slab and its **own** arena inside the C
+allocator (`libvccl_arena_allocator.so`). Pools are created explicitly with a
+`register_kind` declaring how their slab should be registered on NCCL
+communicators (zero-copy buffer registration, symmetric-window registration,
+or none). Allocations inside `with vccl_alloc.in_pool(name):` are routed
+through PyTorch's caching layer into the matching slab.
+
+## Package Layout
+
+```
+vccl_alloc/
+├── __init__.py                    # Public API re-exports
+├── setup.py                       # Module-load setup, ctypes bindings, registry
+├── vccl_arena_allocator.cu        # C++ allocator (compiles to libvccl_arena_allocator.so)
+├── demo.py                        # End-to-end test of the public API
+└── README.md                      # This file
+```
+
+## Public API
+
+All symbols below are importable as `vccl_alloc.<name>` after
+`import vccl_alloc`.
+
+### Lifecycle
+
+| Symbol | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `create_pool(name, slot_size=None, num_slots=None, register_kind=REG_BUFFER)` | Allocate a NCCL slab and create a `MemPool` for it. |
+| `destroy_pool(name)` | Drain the pool, deregister from comms, return slab to NCCL. |
+| `in_pool(name)` | Context manager: route allocations into the named pool. |
+| `register_for_collectives(comm)` | Register every existing pool's slab on `comm`. Idempotent. |
+| `deregister_collectives()` | Reverse of the above; called automatically at exit. |
+
+### Getters / Diagnostics
+
+| Symbol | Returns |
+|---|---|
+| `get_pool(name)` | The `torch.cuda.MemPool`, or `None`. |
+| `get_pool_backing(name)` | Raw `nccl.mem_alloc` result (NcclBuffer / int). |
+| `get_pool_slot_size(name)` | Bytes per slot. |
+| `get_pool_slot_num(name)` | Total slot count. |
+| `get_pool_slot_addr(name, idx)` | Device pointer of slot `idx` as `int`. |
+| `list_pools()` | All registered pool names. |
+| `live_count(name)` | Currently-live slot count from the C side. |
+| `peak_live(name)` | Peak live slot count since the pool was created. |
+| `is_enabled()` | `False` when `VCCL_ALLOC_DISABLE` is set; everything else is no-op. |
+
+### Zero-overhead slot tensors
+
+| Symbol | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `get_slot_byte_tensor(name, idx)` | Pre-built uint8 CUDA tensor backing slot `idx`. Same Python object every call. |
+| `get_slot_tensor(name, idx, shape, dtype)` | Slot `idx` viewed as `(shape, dtype)`. Pure views, no allocation. |
+| `acquire_slot_byte_tensor(name)` | Atomically claim a free slot; raises if all slots are held. Search starts at `cursor++ % num_slots`. |
+| `acquire_slot_tensor(name, shape, dtype)` | `acquire_slot_byte_tensor` + `(shape, dtype)` view. |
+| `release_slot(name, tensor)` | Free a previously-acquired slot. `tensor` may be the byte tensor or any view of it. |
+
+**Acquire / release contract**: `acquire_*` marks a slot in-use and refuses to return another until somebody calls `release_slot` on it. Forgetting to release will eventually hit `RuntimeError: pool 'X' exhausted`. Use the `get_*` variants when you don't need explicit lifetime tracking; use `acquire_*` + `release_slot` when you do.
+
+Opt in by passing `prebuild_slot_tensors=True` to `create_pool`. At pool creation the module runs `torch.empty(slot_size, dtype=uint8)` once per slot through the pool's own pluggable allocator, then holds the resulting tensors for the pool's lifetime. At use time, `get_slot_byte_tensor` is a list lookup (~0.2 µs); `get_slot_tensor(shape, dtype)` adds slice + 2 view ops (~1–3 µs total). Compare to ~9–13 µs for `with in_pool(...): torch.empty(...)`.
+
+The two contracts (pre-built shell tensors vs `with in_pool(...)` dynamic alloc) are **mutually exclusive per pool** — pre-building permanently claims every slot, so dynamic allocations on the same pool will OOM. Pick one at `create_pool` time. If you need both, create two pools.
+
+```python
+vccl_alloc.create_pool(
+    "fast_main_buf", slot_size=20 << 20, num_slots=8,
+    register_kind=vccl_alloc.REG_BUFFER,
+    prebuild_slot_tensors=True,
+)
+
+# Hot path — replaces `with in_pool(...): torch.empty(...)`:
+buf = vccl_alloc.get_slot_tensor("fast_main_buf", idx, shape=(B, H), dtype=torch.bfloat16)
+```
+
+Use this path when:
+
+- Your slots have known fixed addresses (the case for vccl_alloc — that's the whole point).
+- You don't need the dynamic alloc/free behavior of `with in_pool(...)` for that pool.
+- You're calling per-step on the hot path (dispatch / combine).
+
+Do **not** use this path when:
+
+- The pool also needs to back dynamic `torch.empty(...)` calls (it can't — slots are claimed forever).
+- You expect to release individual slots and re-allocate. Lifetime is bounded by `destroy_pool`, not by tensor refcounts.
+
+### Constants
+
+| Symbol | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `REG_BUFFER` | `ncclCommRegister` — zero-copy IB / NVLink fast path. |
+| `REG_WINDOW` | `ncclCommWindowRegister` — one-sided / NVLS / SHARP. |
+| `REG_NONE`   | No NCCL registration; useful for testing or non-collective pools. |
+| `DEFAULT_SLOT_SIZE` | From `VCCL_ALLOC_SLOT_SIZE` env var or 66 MiB. |
+| `DEFAULT_NUM_SLOTS` | From `VCCL_ALLOC_NUM_SLOTS` env var or 64. |
+| `LOCAL_RANK` | The CUDA device this module bound to at import time. |
+
+### Environment Variables
+
+| Variable | Effect |
+|---|---|
+| `VCCL_ALLOC_DISABLE` | Truthy → all functions become no-ops. |
+| `VCCL_ALLOC_LIB` | Path to `libvccl_arena_allocator.so` (default: same dir). |
+| `VCCL_ALLOC_SLOT_SIZE` | Default `slot_size` for `create_pool`. |
+| `VCCL_ALLOC_NUM_SLOTS` | Default `num_slots` for `create_pool`. |
+| `VCCL_MOE_PATH` | Prepended to `sys.path` so `import nccl.core` resolves. |
+| `LOCAL_RANK` | Standard `torchrun` variable; selects the CUDA device. |
+
+## Typical Usage
+
+```python
+import vccl_alloc
+
+# At setup (anywhere, before pools are used):
+vccl_alloc.create_pool(
+    "kv_cache",
+    slot_size=66 * 1024 * 1024,
+    num_slots=64,
+    register_kind=vccl_alloc.REG_BUFFER,
+)
+vccl_alloc.create_pool(
+    "nvls_buf",
+    slot_size=66 * 1024 * 1024,
+    num_slots=8,
+    register_kind=vccl_alloc.REG_WINDOW,
+)
+
+# After init_process_group:
+vccl_alloc.register_for_collectives(dp_comm)
+vccl_alloc.register_for_collectives(tp_comm)
+
+# Anywhere downstream:
+with vccl_alloc.in_pool("kv_cache"):
+    cache = torch.empty(B, H, T, D, device='cuda')
+
+# Process exit teardown is automatic via atexit.
+```
+
+## How to Run
+
+    # Prerequisite: build the .so once
+    cd vccl_alloc/
+    nvcc -Xcompiler -fPIC -shared -std=c++17 \
+         -o libvccl_arena_allocator.so vccl_arena_allocator.cu
+
+    # Run from anywhere via -m
+    cd ..
+    python -m vccl_alloc.demo
+
+    # Or directly (sys.path is patched at top of demo.py)
+    python vccl_alloc/demo.py
+
+Output ends with `All demo tests passed.` and a note about the comm-dependent paths. Each test uses `assert` on its key invariants, so failures are loud.
+
+## What the Demo Covers
+
+`demo.py` exercises every public symbol re-exported via `__init__.py`
+**except** `register_for_collectives` / `deregister_collectives` — those
+require a real NCCL `Communicator` and are tested end-to-end by `VCCLManager`
+during real distributed runs. The 12 demo cases:
+
+1. `create_pool` + `in_pool` route allocations into the slab
+2. Getter API (`list_pools`, `get_pool`, `get_pool_slot_size`, `get_pool_slot_num`, `get_pool_slot_addr`, `get_pool_backing`) — including unknown-name and out-of-range edge cases
+3. Ring slot allocation pattern (cursor advances forward, doesn't reuse freed middle slot)
+4. `live_count` / `peak_live` diagnostics
+5. Slot exhaustion raises `OutOfMemoryError`
+6. `storage.resize_(0)` / `resize_(N)` round-trip stays in the slab
+7. Oversized request rejected by `my_malloc`
+8. `in_pool('unknown')` raises `KeyError`
+9. Multiple pools coexist with independent slabs and slot sizes
+10. Duplicate `create_pool(name=...)` raises `ValueError`
+11. Invalid `register_kind` raises `ValueError`
+12. `destroy_pool` releases bookkeeping; unknown name is a no-op
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- A working CUDA device.
+- `nccl4py` importable as `nccl.core` (set `VCCL_MOE_PATH` if needed).
+- `libvccl_arena_allocator.so` built next to `setup.py` (see *How to Run*).
+- PyTorch ≥ 2.5 (`torch.cuda.MemPool`, `CUDAPluggableAllocator`).
+
+## Notes
+
+- **Allocator backing**: `vccl_alloc.create_pool` always calls
+  `nccl.mem_alloc(slot_size * num_slots)` for its slab. There is no
+  Python-visible "use this externally-allocated pointer" path; all slabs are
+  NCCL-allocated by design.
+- **Window registration is method-on-comm**: vccl_alloc tries the module-level
+  `nccl.comm_window_register` first and falls back to `comm.register_window`
+  (which is the layout used by VCCL_MOE's nccl4py).
+- **At-exit teardown** drains every pool, deregisters every (pool, comm)
+  pair, calls `nccl.mem_free`, and shuts down the C allocator. The `.so` is
+  loaded with `RTLD_NODELETE` so straggling frees during interpreter
+  finalization don't crash on a missing function pointer.
